# GeoTrust TLS RSA CA G1 — the intermediate yugiohblog.konami.com does not send.
#
# The server presents only its leaf certificate, so the chain to DigiCert
# Global Root G2 cannot be built. Browsers and macOS curl paper over this by
# fetching the intermediate from the URL in the leaf's AIA extension; Python
# does not, so verification fails outright.
#
# Committed rather than fetched at runtime: fetching a certificate and then
# trusting it is a trust decision made by whoever answers that request. This
# is a fixed, public CA certificate, checked in and reviewable.
#
# Subject: C=US, O=DigiCert Inc, CN=GeoTrust TLS RSA CA G1
# Issuer : C=US, O=DigiCert Inc, CN=DigiCert Global Root G2
# Expires: 2027-11-02  — a test fails once this is within 90 days
# Source : http://cacerts.geotrust.com/GeoTrustTLSRSACAG1.crt (AIA of the leaf)
# SHA-256: c06e307f7cfc1d32fa72a4c033c87b90019af216f0775d64978a2eca6c8a230e
#
# The fingerprint is asserted by the tests. Swapping this file for another
# certificate would otherwise pass review unless someone read the base64 by
# eye, and it is loaded as a trust anchor -- so a substitution would decide
# what the scraper accepts.
